This is my port of dvips to run under OS9/68k.The only trick to compiling is the need for popen()/pclose(), whicharen't part of the Microware C library.  If you have TOP's os9lib.l orsome other library, you should be able to just compile by editing theLIBS= line in the makefile.  If you don't, then edit "system.c" byuncommenting the stub definitions for popen() and pclose().  You'lllose the ability to send the output into another program, but that'sabout it.Several of the files which are built automatically by the U**x makefileswere constructed by hand.  Don't do anything stronger than "make clean"unless you want to reconstruct them yourself.Other than the addition of system.c and system.h, the only major changesI made were to search.c and pathsrch.c, to add the %-modifiers that aredocumented in the manual.Note that Thomas Rokicki's original dvips source is public domain,but Karl Berry's changes are not, they're covered by the GNU GeneralPublic License.  My changes are also public domain.The hassle with the GNU GPL is that you can't give away this program withoutalso giving away the source.  The only loophole is that you can simply tellthe person you give it to how to get the source.  The answer:   The Unix source is available from numerous Internet FTP sites.   This source is available on Delphi's OS9 SIG.